<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Grand Principality of Serbia</h1><p class="st-meta">1091 CE – 1217 CE (approx.) · capital Ras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Medieval Serbian state of Raška, where Stefan Nemanja united the Serbian lands and founded the dynasty that ruled Serbia for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a> declined in the late 11th century, Raška in the inland highlands gained independence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-zupan">Grand Župan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukan-of-raska">Vukan</a>, who from about 1091 raided Byzantine territory. His successors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukanovic-dynasty">Vukanović</a> line ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a>, often as Byzantine or Hungarian clients.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The grand župan ruled with regional lords (župans) and an assembly of nobles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> (1166–1196) defeated his brothers, annexed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/travunia">Travunia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a> around 1186, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/studenica-monastery">Studenica</a> monastery. His youngest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Rastko</a>, became the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and helped establish Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monasticism at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/monastic-community-of-mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. Byzantine and Hungarian pressure shaped its politics.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Nemanja</a> abdicated in 1196 in favour of his son Stefan, who received a royal crown from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a> in 1217. The state became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/grand-principality-of-serbia.jpg" alt="Grand Principality of Serbia" width="320" height="356"><figcaption>Image: Boban Markovic · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serbie_de_Nemanja_1184.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1091 CE – 1217 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vukan, Uroš I, Stefan Nemanja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Raška, Rascia, Grand Župan's Serbia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1154&amp;focus=grand-principality-of-serbia" aria-label="Grand Principality of Serbia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5172 647 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Grand Principality of Serbia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5492 903L5428 912M5492 903L5444 916M5492 903L5464 935M5492 903L5709 970M5492 903L5521 939"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5428 912h0M5444 916h0M5464 935h0M5709 970h0M5521 939h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 903h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 903h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1154&amp;focus=grand-principality-of-serbia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
